From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ai_re <ai_re@localhost>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: add the Annapurna Labs Alpine V2 integrated Ethernet
 (al_eth)

A DM Ethernet driver for PCI 1c36:0001 (Ethernet unit revision 2) on
Annapurna's HAL. The HAL subset (eth main, UDMA main/config, IOFIC,
UDMA IOFIC and their headers) is the al_eth Linux driver's, unmodified,
and not part of this patch: the ai_re build copies those 23 files in
from packages/al-eth/src after patching (vendor in series.nix), so the
repo holds one copy. al_hal_plat_services.h and al_hal_plat_types.h are
U-Boot shims.
HAL assertions print the condition and latch a flag instead of stopping
the board; the driver then refuses to touch the DMA until the next start.

the board params say), fixed link, one polled TX and RX queue on UDMA 0.
No SerDes, no link management, no interrupts. The port's mode comes from
the board params, which the handoff code (alpine_v2_eth_handoff_one())
has made sure exist; PHY-bearing or SerDes ports are refused at probe.

Sequence as the Linux al_eth driver: start() first quiesces whatever ran
before (MAC stop, all eight queues reset, UDMAs disabled, FLR with board
params and MAC kept across it), then adapter init, MAC and link config,
queue config, RX forwarding (unicast to our MAC + broadcast, drop the
rest), 63 RX buffers, MAC start. stop() and remove() (DM_FLAG_OS_PREPARE:
before the OS) quiesce again, so Linux finds an idle function that still
carries its params and MAC. Probe only maps BARs, runs the handoff and
allocates; the data path is untouched until a network command, so a
fault there cannot get in the way of a disk boot.

DMA buffers and rings are cached memory with explicit cache maintenance;
TX goes through an aligned bounce buffer padded to 60 bytes; TX
completions come from the head register; the MAC's link bit is reported,
not trusted. The ROM MAC is what the handoff left in forwarding entry 0;
write_hwaddr writes entry 0, which is both the RX filter and the address
Linux reads. Devices are named al_eth<PCI device>, as the vendor U-Boot
does (al_eth1 for 00:01.0).

Scope: alpine-v2.
